    "passage": "Wasting food has far-reaching effects, both in the U.S. and around the world. In the United States, up to 40% of all food made is never eaten, [[1]] about 95% of that wasted food ends up in landfills. Globally, one-third of all food produced is discarded, adding pressure to the environment. Experts believe that cutting food waste [[2]] 15% could help feed over 25 million Americans every year.\nAlthough food waste starts long before it reaches the home kitchen, you can help minimize waste by adopting strategies such as smartly shopping, properly [[3]] food, or creatively repurposing leftovers or overripe fruits. A good first step is to plan your weekly meals and make shopping lists. Try to avoid [[4]] purchases just because they’re on sale, especially if you don’t normally eat them. In your refrigerator and pantry, move older foods to the front and write “eat by” dates on containers using masking tape. If fruit starts to go soft or turn brown, you can make it into smoothies or baked goods. [[5]] the food shows no clear signs of going bad, it’s often still fine to use. And you’ll save quite a bit of money too.",
